## Six indexing signals, one flag
While the closed beta runs, this app defers to the marketing site and must say so in SIX places at once:
1. the `robots` meta — `noindex, follow`;
2. `robots.txt` — `Disallow: /`, and no `Sitemap:` line;
3. `/sitemap.xml` — 404, NOT an empty `<urlset>` (Search Console keeps an empty one, reports it as an error, and re-fetches it);
4. the canonical on `/` — the marketing site;
5. every other page's canonical — absent, because `metadataBase` is the marketing site then and a relative canonical would name a URL that does not exist there;
6. the `Link: rel="alternate" hreflang` RESPONSE HEADERS next-intl publishes from the proxy — off. Google honours these exactly as it honours the head tags, and nothing that reads page source can see them.

`NEXT_PUBLIC_ALLOW_INDEXING=1` must move all six together. Flag any change that moves one without the others, and any new signal that does not read the flag at all — that is how the sitemap came to advertise the app's URLs on the same deploy that sent `Disallow: /`. They contradicted each other once already, and Google resolves a contradiction by guessing.

The flag is `NEXT_PUBLIC_`, so it is baked in by `next build`. Setting it at test time changes what the tests expect and nothing about what they test.

## Two lists, deliberately different
`PUBLIC_PATHS` is what the proxy lets through without a session. `INDEXABLE_PATHS` is what the sitemap publishes and what `robots.ts` subtracts from the route tree. A page can be reachable without a session and still be absent from the sitemap on purpose — `/demarrer` is exactly that. Adding a public route means deciding about both; flag a PR that decides only one.

## Metadata inheritance
Next merges metadata SHALLOWLY from the root layout down, so a page that sets no `alternates` inherits the root's — which names the root. That made every chapter tell a crawler its canonical URL was the homepage. Any new page needs its own `alternates`, and any derived list (the robots disallow rules) must stay derived rather than becoming a hand-maintained list, which is what drifted twice.
